@page "/docs/common/data"

@* Data and filters: the request a data component hands its provider and the result it gets back,
   loading from EF Core, filters in code, the operators, the ?filter= syntax, fields, calling an API
   from WebAssembly, OData endpoints, and failed loads. From the data foundations design
   (artifacts/izzyblazor-data-foundations-design.md) and Core/Data. *@

<ArticlePage Section="common" Slug="data">
    <Lead>
        A <Code>Table</Code> with an <Code>ItemsProvider</Code> asks for its rows with a <Code>DataRequest</Code>: the range,
        the sort and the filter. You answer with a <Code>DataResult</Code>. The request applies itself to an EF Core query,
        travels to an API as a query string or JSON, and never turns a value into code.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">The request and the result</Heading>
        <TextBlock>The request says which rows the component wants:</TextBlock>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Member</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>Skip</code>, <code>Take</code></td><td>The range: rows to skip, and how many to take (null for every row). <code>Page</code> gives the range as a page number, from 1.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Sort</code></td><td>A list of <code>SortField</code>s, a key and whether it's descending. A Table sorts by one column.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Filter</code></td><td>The filter tree, or null. A Table's is <code>Filter.All</code> of one condition per filtered column, and any other conditions of a bound <code>Filter</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Search</code></td><td>Free text to look for in the searchable fields. A Table doesn't set it; a provider can, from your own search box: <code>request with { Search = query }</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Cursor</code></td><td>When the component pages by cursor, the <code>Next</code> or <code>Previous</code> of an earlier result.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>NeedsTotal</code></td><td>Whether to count. False when the component pages by cursor, where the total is optional.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>CancellationToken</code></td><td>Canceled when a newer request replaces this one. Pass it on.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <TextBlock>
            The result is the rows, with the <Code>Total</Code> that numbered pages need, or the <Code>Next</Code> and
            <Code>Previous</Code> cursors:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp">@Result</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">From a database</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The simplest way needs no provider: pass an EF Core query as a Table's <Code>Items</Code>, and the database
            sorts, filters and pages it. Write an <Code>ItemsProvider</Code> when you need more, such as scoping by the
            signed-in user per request, an API in between, or your own search.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            In a provider, <Code>ToResultAsync</Code> filters, searches and sorts a query, counts what the filter keeps (only when
            <Code>NeedsTotal</Code>), and lists the range. It takes EF Core's async methods as arguments, so IzzyBlazor
            doesn't depend on EF Core:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" HighlightLines="7-9">@FromDatabase</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            The request carries the component's columns, which say how each key reads an item, so the provider never maps
            keys to properties. Each condition becomes a LINQ expression on the column's own property, with its value as a
            query parameter, and the database does the work. For a list in memory, <Code>request.ToResult(list.AsQueryable())</Code>
            does the same. <Code>request.Apply(query)</Code> filters, sorts and takes the range without counting;
            <Code>ApplyFiltering</Code> and <Code>ApplySorting</Code> do one part each.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            If the provider throws, the Table logs the exception and shows "Couldn't load the rows." with Retry; see
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/table#when-loading-fails">When loading fails</Anchor>. A request it canceled
            isn't an error.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Filters in code</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A filter is a tree: a condition on one field (<Code>FieldFilter</Code>), conditions joined by and or or
            (<Code>FilterGroup</Code>), or the opposite of one (<Code>NotFilter</Code>). Fields are named by their key, the
            same key the address uses (<Code>?f-status=</Code> filters the field <Code>status</Code>): a column's property name
            in lower case, or its <Code>SortKey</Code> (see <Anchor Href="docs/common/data#fields">Fields</Anchor>).
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp">@InCode</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Bind a Table's <Code>Filter</Code> to set it from code on an interactive page. Each condition a column's panel can
            show (contains for text, between, at least, at most or equals for a number, on day for a date, equals or in for a choice) shows there; any
            other, such as an or group, still applies and shows as a tag that removes it. With an <Code>ItemsProvider</Code>, the
            whole filter reaches it as <Code>request.Filter</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">@Bound</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            A provider that passes conditions on to an API reads one with <Code>Find</Code>: the condition on that field that
            every match must meet, or null.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp">@Find</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">Operators</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Which operators a field takes depends on its type. An operator its type doesn't take matches nothing.</TextBlock>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Operators</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Text</td><td><code>Equals</code>, <code>NotEquals</code>, <code>Contains</code>, <code>DoesNotContain</code>, <code>StartsWith</code>, <code>EndsWith</code>, <code>In</code>, <code>NotIn</code>, <code>IsEmpty</code>, <code>IsNotEmpty</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Numbers, times</td><td><code>Equals</code>, <code>NotEquals</code>, <code>LessThan</code>, <code>LessThanOrEqual</code>, <code>GreaterThan</code>, <code>GreaterThanOrEqual</code>, <code>Between</code>, <code>In</code>, <code>NotIn</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Dates</td><td>The same, and <code>OnDay</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Enums, booleans, Guids</td><td><code>Equals</code>, <code>NotEquals</code>, <code>In</code>, <code>NotIn</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Anything that can be null</td><td>Also <code>IsNull</code> and <code>IsNotNull</code></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Text ignores case</strong>, as a Table's filters do. Set <Code>MatchCase</Code> on the condition to match
                it. <Code>IsEmpty</Code> matches empty text and null; <Code>DoesNotContain</Code> keeps null.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Text ignores case</strong> by lowering both sides, which a database index can't serve.
                <Code>FilterCase.Database</Code> on a Table or DataGrid, or as a field's <Code>Case</Code>, compares text as it is and
                leaves it to the database's collation; <Code>FilterCase.MatchCase</Code> makes case count. The filter's text
                doesn't carry this, so an endpoint that reads it, such as an export, sets <Code>Case</Code> on its own fields.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Values are parsed for the field's type</strong>, so <Code>"10"</Code> filters a number and
                <Code>"Paid"</Code> an enum. Numbers are read as the person writes them in the current culture
                (<Code>"1.234,5"</Code> in German), then in the invariant form (<Code>"1234.5"</Code>); dates and times in ISO
                form (<Code>"2026-10-28"</Code>), then the current culture's. A value that doesn't
                parse matches nothing, as does a condition on a field that doesn't exist.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Number bounds round the way the comparison means.</strong> On a whole-number field, "at least 2.5" is
                3 or more and "at most 2.5" is 2 or less. A bound beyond what the type holds keeps every row or none.
                <Code>Between</Code> includes both ends, and a null end has no limit.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong><Code>OnDay</Code> matches a day.</strong> A <Code>DateTime</Code> keeps the day's range, so an index
                can serve it; a <Code>DateTimeOffset</Code> compares the day on the clock where it was recorded.
            </ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Filters as text</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>Filter.Parse</Code> reads a filter written in a subset of OData's <Code>$filter</Code>, and a filter's
            <Code>ToString()</Code> writes one. It's the form the request's query string uses for <Code>?filter=</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="text">@Syntax</Code>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Write</th><th>For</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>eq ne gt ge lt le</code></td><td>Comparisons; <code>eq null</code> and <code>ne null</code> for the null checks</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>status in ('Paid','Pending')</code></td><td>Any of the values; <code>not (status in (...))</code> for none</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>customer contains 'ada'</code></td><td>Also <code>contains(customer,'ada')</code>, and <code>startswith</code>, <code>endswith</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>date(placed) eq 2026-10-28</code></td><td>On that day</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>and or not ( )</code></td><td>Groups; <code>and</code> binds before <code>or</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>'O''Brien'</code>, <code>-1.5</code>, <code>true</code>, <code>2026-10-28T09:30:00Z</code></td><td>Values: text in single quotes (a quote doubled), numbers, true or false, dates and times, Guids</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <TextBlock>
            Parsing only builds the tree: names are field keys and values stay values, so
            <Code>name eq 'x'' or 1 eq 1'</Code> looks for that exact text. Text that isn't in this form doesn't parse
            (<Code>TryParse</Code> returns false), and a request read from a query string with such a filter matches nothing.
            Text ignores case here too, unlike OData's own <Code>contains</Code>. <Code>MatchCase</Code> isn't written, so a
            condition sent this way ignores case on the server; send the request as JSON to keep it.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Fields</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A field is a key, the expression that reads it, and what can be done with it: sorted, filtered, searched, with
            which operators. A Table's columns are its fields. A <Code>PropertyColumn</Code>'s key is its property path in
            lower case (<Code>customer.name</Code>) unless it has a <Code>SortKey</Code>; a <Code>TemplateColumn</Code>'s is its
            <Code>SortKey</Code>, and it filters and searches by its <Code>FilterBy</Code> and sorts by its <Code>SortBy</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Where there are no columns, <Code>DataFields.Of&lt;T&gt;()</Code> reads them from a type: each public top-level
            property a filter can compare, keyed by its name in lower case, sortable and filterable, with text searchable
            and an enum's names as its choices. It reads <Code>[Display(Name)]</Code> for the caption,
            <Code>[DisplayFormat]</Code> and <Code>[DataType]</Code> for the format, and leaves out
            <Code>[ScaffoldColumn(false)]</Code> and <Code>[Display(AutoGenerateField = false)]</Code> properties. Or declare
            fields yourself:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp">@Declared</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>Operators</Code> narrows what a field takes. <Code>Filter</Code> builds its condition yourself, for a computed
            value or one stored differently from how it's shown: it gets the condition with its values parsed for the field's
            type, and returns the filter on the item, or null for the usual one. It runs for every operator, so check the one
            you handle; and it matches case unless you lower both sides. A Table column takes the same <Code>Filter</Code>
            parameter.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">@Custom</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">From the browser to an API</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            In a WebAssembly or Auto app the provider runs in the browser, so it sends the request to an API.
            <Code>ToQueryString()</Code> writes it for a GET (<Code>sort=-placed&amp;skip=20&amp;take=10&amp;filter=total ge 100</Code>,
            shown unescaped, with <Code>total=false</Code> when it doesn't need the count), and the request also serializes with
            <Code>System.Text.Json</Code> for a POST. On the server, <Code>DataRequest&lt;T&gt;.Parse</Code> reads it back, and
            <Code>ToResultAsync</Code> with the fields you declare applies it:
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Both sides use the same row type, here <Code>OrderRow</Code> in a project the client and the server share:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Orders.razor (client)">@Client</Code>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="Program.cs (server)" HighlightLines="8,11">@Server</Code>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>The keys are the client's columns.</strong> The server's fields need the same keys. With the same row
                type they match; a column on a path, <Code>o =&gt; o.Customer.Name</Code>, is <Code>customer.name</Code>, so give
                it the <Code>SortKey</Code> of the server's field.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Only declared fields can be reached.</strong> A condition on a key that isn't a filterable field matches
                nothing, and a sort by one is left out. <Code>DataFields.Of&lt;T&gt;()</Code> declares every public property,
                so read it from a type made for the API, not an entity with a password hash on it.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Cap the range.</strong> The request asks for its own <Code>Take</Code>, or every row without one.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Large or deep filters match nothing.</strong> A filter of more than 1,000 nodes (conditions, groups and nots), or
                nested more than 64 deep, matches no rows rather than building an expression that would overflow the
                server's stack. The text form nests at most 32 deep.
            </ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">OData endpoints</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            When the API is an OData endpoint, <Code>ToODataQuery()</Code> writes the request as its query options:
            <Code>$filter</Code>, <Code>$orderby</Code>, <Code>$skip</Code>, <Code>$top</Code>, <Code>$count</Code> and
            <Code>$search</Code>. OData compares text with case, so text that ignores case is written lower-cased on both
            sides (<Code>contains(tolower(customer),'ada')</Code>), and paths use <Code>/</Code>
            (<Code>customer/name</Code>). Values are written as their fields' types: a Table's request carries its fields, and
            for a request you read with <Code>Parse</Code>, pass them, <Code>ToODataQuery(fields)</Code>. Keys are lower case;
            if the service matches property names with case, give the columns a <Code>SortKey</Code> with its names. A cursor
            isn't part of OData: pass
            <Code>request.Cursor</Code> on yourself.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp">@OData</Code>

